        titleSanskrit: 'Nāda-Anusandhāna — Contemplation of the Inner Sound',
        content: 'Nāda is the inner sound — the subtle vibration that the practitioner begins to hear when external sounds are excluded and the mind is sufficiently still. The Aghori tradition maps ten stages of nāda, from the gross to the subtle.\n\nThe first stages are accessible to any sincere practitioner who has completed the 40-day preparation: after Mānasika Japa, sit in complete stillness. Block the ears with the thumbs (Śānmuṅkī Mudrā). You will begin to hear a high-pitched ringing or humming. This is not tinnitus — it is the first perceivable layer of nāda, called the Parā Nāda. Focus on it. As concentration deepens, the sound will change — becoming more musical, more complex, more enveloping.\n\nThe tradition describes the progression through ten sounds: chini (like crickets), chini-chini (like bells), ghantā (bell), conch (śaṅkha), tāṃḍavī (drum), bherī (large drum), mṛdaṅga (barrel drum), veṇā (flute), bāṃśī (bamboo flute), and finally the anāhata dhvani — the "unstruck sound" that is not produced by any physical cause. This final sound is the vibration of pure consciousness itself.\n\nThe Aghori does not pursue these sounds as experiences to be collected. Each is a signpost indicating the depth of absorption. The practice is to hear the sound without grasping, without analyzing, without comparing it to anything. Simply hear. The sound and the listener eventually become indistinguishable — and in that moment, the duality of subject and object dissolves. [TRADITIONAL]',
