        titleSanskrit: 'Bhairava — The Terrifying One Who Is Not Terrible',
        content: 'Bhairava is the fierce form of Śiva — but "fierce" in the tantric sense does not mean cruel or destructive. It means uncompromising. Bhairava is consciousness that refuses to be comforted, that strips away every illusion, that stands naked in the cremation ground and dares you to join it.\n\nThe word derives from bhiru (fearful) and bhāva (being/existence) — literally, "the state of fear." But the Aghori interpretation reverses this: Bhairava is not the one who creates fear. Bhairava is the one who IS fear, who has become one with what the mind most resists, and who has discovered that on the other side of every fear is a freedom that cannot be described.\n\nThe tradition recognizes eight primary forms of Bhairava (Aṣṭāṅga Bhairava): Kāla Bhairava (Lord of Time), Asitāṅga Bhairava (Dark-Limbed), Ruru Bhairava (The Fierce Dog), Caṇḍa Bhairava (The Violent), Krodha Bhairava (The Wrathful), Unmatta Bhairava (The Mad/Enraptured), Kapāla Bhairava (The Skull-Bearer), and Bhīṣaṇa Bhairava (The Terrifying). Each form governs a specific aspect of the ego\'s resistance. Kāla Bhairava, the most widely worshipped in the Aghori lineages, governs the fear of death and the illusion of time. His vehicle is a dog — the most loyal of animals, and the one most associated with the cremation ground, where dogs are often the only living beings present at night.\n\nThe iconography is precise: Bhairava is depicted with matted locks, a garland of skulls, a trident (triśūla — the three gunas transcended), a drum (ḍamaru — the rhythm of creation and destruction), and a begging bowl (kapaāla — the skull, signifying that consciousness has no needs). He is often shown standing on a corpse (representing the ego) or seated on a dog (representing loyalty beyond social status). His consort is Bhairavī — the fierce form of Śakti who is the active power of Bhairava\'s consciousness. [TRADITIONAL]',
