        titleSanskrit: 'Sevā — The Service That Dissolves the Ego',
        content: 'The Aghori\'s final practice — and in many ways the most important — is Sevā (selfless service). This is not charity performed for spiritual merit. It is the natural expression of the non-dual state: when you no longer see yourself as separate from others, serving them is not a virtuous act — it is the only possible response.\n\nAghoreshwar Bhagwan Ramji established this principle at the center of his Aghor lineage: the ashram in Varanasi serves leprosy patients, provides food to the hungry, and offers medical care to those who cannot afford it. The Aghori touches the untouchable — literally, with bare hands — because in the Aghori\'s direct experience, there is no "untouchable." The skin disease, the social stigma, the fear of contagion — all are real at the conventional level. But the Aghori operates from a level where these distinctions do not exist.\n\nFor the modern practitioner, Sevā does not require running a leprosy clinic. It requires finding the place where your unique capacities meet the world\'s needs — and serving there without expectation of return. The tradition identifies three forms of Sevā: Dāna (giving — material support to those in need), Śravaṇa (listening — being present for those who need to be heard), and Kāyaka (physical work — labor that benefits others without expectation of recognition).\n\nThe ego-dissolving mechanism of Sevā is straightforward: every act of service without expectation of return weakens the ego\'s claim that it is the center of the universe. The ego says "I serve, therefore I am good." The Aghori says "service happens, and the one who claims credit for it is a ghost." [TRADITIONAL + ORAL]',
