        content: 'The spiritual landscape — both in India and globally — is saturated with claims: this guru has siddhis, this practice gives powers, this ashram performs miracles, this yantra grants wealth. The Aghori tradition insists on Viveka (discernment) as the final and perhaps most important practice.\n\nThe tradition distinguishes four categories of evidence: traditional (attested across multiple lineages and textual sources), oral (preserved in living guru-śiṣya transmission), field (compiled from practitioner field manuals and direct observation), and reconstructed (assembled from fragmentary sources — may differ from specific living lineages). This course has applied this grading throughout.\n\nThe practitioner must develop the ability to distinguish: documented traditional practice from later folklore; authentic lineage transmission from self-proclaimed guruhood; verifiable experiential reports from unverifiable siddhi claims; the psychology of spiritual experience from genuine transformation. The criterion is always the same: does this practice or claim dissolve the ego, or does it strengthen it? Genuine sādhanā produces humility, compassion, and simplicity. Inauthentic spirituality produces pride, exclusivity, and complexity.\n\nThe Aghori is particularly skeptical of: claims of supernatural powers used for personal gain, practices that require large sums of money, gurus who demand absolute obedience without transparency, and any system that promises results without consistent effort. The tradition\'s own practices — including the most extreme ones — are always offered freely, always accompanied by warnings, and always oriented toward liberation rather than power.\n\nThis discernment is not cynicism. It is the application of the witness consciousness — Sākṣī Bhāva — to the spiritual marketplace. The same capacity that observes the mind\'s movements during meditation can observe the spiritual world\'s claims during daily life. [TRADITIONAL + ORAL]',
